
---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Garden Care | Silken Valley Lifestyle"
  description="Practical gardening advice covering soil preparation, composting, watering, pruning, propagation, pest management, and seasonal garden maintenance."
  heroTitle="Garden Care"
  heroText="Practical knowledge and seasonal guidance for maintaining a healthy, beautiful garden."
  heroCompact={true}
>
  <section class="care-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Garden Maintenance & Practical Advice</p>
    <h1>Caring for Your Garden Throughout the Year</h1>

    <p>
      A beautiful garden requires thoughtful care, regular
      attention, and an understanding of the natural
      environment. Healthy soil, appropriate watering,
      careful pruning, and seasonal maintenance all
      contribute to a thriving outdoor space.
    </p>

    <p>
      Whether tending a small courtyard, maintaining
      established borders, or caring for a larger landscape,
      understanding the fundamentals of garden care helps
      plants flourish and keeps the garden looking its best
      throughout the seasons.
    </p>
  </section>

  <nav class="care-nav" aria-label="Garden care sections">
    <a href="#soil">Soil & Compost</a>
    <a href="#watering">Watering</a>
    <a href="#mulching">Mulching</a>
    <a href="#pruning">Pruning</a>
    <a href="#propagation">Propagation</a>
    <a href="#pests">Pests & Diseases</a>
    <a href="#weeds">Weed Management</a>
    <a href="#seasonal">Seasonal Care</a>
    <a href="#okanagan">Okanagan Gardening</a>
  </nav>

  <section class="care-section" id="soil">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Foundation of a Healthy Garden</p>
    <h2>Soil Preparation & Improvement</h2>

    <p>
      Healthy soil is one of the most important elements
      of successful gardening. It provides plants with
      physical support, water, air, and essential nutrients.
      Understanding the structure and condition of your soil
      helps determine which improvements may be beneficial.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Understanding Soil Types</h3>
        <p>
          Garden soils commonly contain different proportions
          of sand, silt, and clay. Sandy soils drain quickly,
          while clay soils retain more water. Loam combines
          these particles in proportions that often provide
          favourable growing conditions.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Improving Soil Structure</h3>
        <p>
          Organic matter can improve soil structure, encourage
          beneficial organisms, and help regulate moisture.
          Incorporate suitable compost into planting areas
          when preparing or improving garden beds.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Soil Testing</h3>
        <p>
          A soil test can provide useful information about
          pH, nutrient levels, and potential deficiencies.
          Testing helps avoid unnecessary fertilizer
          applications and guides appropriate amendments.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Soil pH</h3>
        <p>
          Soil pH influences the availability of nutrients
          to plants. Some plants prefer acidic conditions,
          while others grow best in neutral or slightly
          alkaline soils. Select plants suited to your
          existing soil whenever possible.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="compost">
    <p class="eyebrow">Recycling Organic Garden Materials</p>
    <h2>Composting & Organic Matter</h2>

    <p>
      Composting transforms suitable garden and kitchen
      materials into a valuable soil amendment. It is a
      practical way to recycle organic matter while
      supporting a healthy garden ecosystem.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Green Materials</h3>
        <p>
          Green materials provide nitrogen and include
          fresh grass clippings, vegetable scraps, fruit
          peelings, and fresh plant trimmings.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Brown Materials</h3>
        <p>
          Brown materials provide carbon and include
          dry leaves, straw, untreated shredded cardboard,
          and small woody garden materials.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Maintaining a Compost Pile</h3>
        <p>
          Combine green and brown materials, maintain
          suitable moisture, and turn the pile periodically
          to encourage airflow and decomposition.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Using Finished Compost</h3>
        <p>
          Mature compost is dark, crumbly, and earthy
          smelling. Add it to garden beds, use it as a
          soil amendment, or apply a thin layer around
          established plants.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="watering">
    <p class="eyebrow">Moisture & Plant Health</p>
    <h2>Watering the Garden</h2>

    <p>
      Water is essential for plant growth, nutrient
      transport, and photosynthesis. The correct watering
      routine depends on plant species, soil type, weather,
      exposure, and the stage of plant establishment.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Deep Watering</h3>
        <p>
          Thorough watering encourages roots to grow
          deeper into the soil. Water slowly enough to
          allow moisture to penetrate the root zone
          rather than running off the surface.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Drip Irrigation</h3>
        <p>
          Drip irrigation delivers water close to plant
          roots. It can reduce evaporation and help
          provide consistent moisture to flower borders,
          vegetable gardens, and shrub beds.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Watering at the Right Time</h3>
        <p>
          Early morning is generally a good time to water.
          It allows moisture to reach the roots before
          the heat of the day and reduces prolonged
          overnight leaf wetness.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Recognizing Water Stress</h3>
        <p>
          Wilting, leaf curling, dry soil, and premature
          leaf drop may indicate insufficient moisture.
          Yellowing leaves and persistently wet soil
          can indicate overwatering or poor drainage.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="mulching">
    <p class="eyebrow">Protecting the Soil</p>
    <h2>Mulching & Ground Cover</h2>

    <p>
      Mulch is applied to the soil surface to conserve
      moisture, moderate soil temperatures, suppress
      weeds, and improve the appearance of garden beds.
      Organic mulches gradually decompose and contribute
      organic matter to the soil.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Organic Mulch</h3>
        <p>
          Shredded bark, wood chips, compost, leaf mould,
          and straw are common organic mulches. Choose
          materials appropriate for the planting area
          and the plants being grown.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Decorative Stone</h3>
        <p>
          Gravel and decorative stone can provide a
          long-lasting surface covering, particularly
          in dry garden designs. Stone does not improve
          soil organic matter and may increase soil
          temperatures in exposed areas.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Applying Mulch</h3>
        <p>
          Apply an even layer over the soil surface.
          Keep mulch away from the stems of perennials
          and the trunks of trees and shrubs to avoid
          excessive moisture against plant tissue.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Living Ground Covers</h3>
        <p>
          Low-growing plants can cover bare soil,
          soften pathways, reduce exposed ground,
          and provide an attractive alternative to
          traditional mulch in suitable locations.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="pruning">
    <p class="eyebrow">Shape, Structure & Plant Health</p>
    <h2>Pruning Trees, Shrubs & Perennials</h2>

    <p>
      Pruning helps maintain plant health, encourage
      suitable growth, remove damaged material, and
      preserve the intended shape of trees and shrubs.
      Different plants require different pruning
      techniques and timing.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Pruning Deciduous Trees</h3>
        <p>
          Remove dead, damaged, and crossing branches
          where appropriate. Young trees benefit from
          developing a strong structure, while established
          trees generally require careful, limited pruning.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Pruning Flowering Shrubs</h3>
        <p>
          Understand whether a shrub flowers on old
          wood or new growth. Spring-flowering shrubs
          often benefit from pruning after flowering,
          while many summer-flowering shrubs can be
          pruned during dormancy or early spring.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Rose Pruning</h3>
        <p>
          Remove dead or damaged canes and shape plants
          according to their rose type. Modern bush roses,
          climbing roses, and old garden roses each have
          different pruning requirements.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Perennial Maintenance</h3>
        <p>
          Remove spent flowers to encourage continued
          flowering in suitable varieties. Cut back
          foliage according to plant requirements and
          leave selected seed heads for winter interest
          and wildlife.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="care-note">
      Always use clean, sharp pruning tools. Avoid
      unnecessary topping of trees and excessive removal
      of healthy branches.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="propagation">
    <p class="eyebrow">Growing New Plants</p>
    <h2>Plant Propagation</h2>

    <p>
      Propagation allows gardeners to increase their
      plant collections, replace older plants, and
      share favourite varieties. Different methods
      are suited to different plant groups.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Growing from Seed</h3>
        <p>
          Seeds are an economical way to grow annuals,
          vegetables, and many perennials. Successful
          germination depends on suitable temperature,
          moisture, light, and sowing depth.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Stem Cuttings</h3>
        <p>
          Cuttings can produce new plants from sections
          of healthy stems. Depending on the species,
          cuttings may be taken from softwood, semi-ripe,
          or hardwood growth.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Division</h3>
        <p>
          Many herbaceous perennials can be divided
          into smaller sections. This helps rejuvenate
          overcrowded clumps and creates additional
          plants for the garden.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Layering</h3>
        <p>
          Layering encourages roots to develop on a
          stem while it remains attached to the parent
          plant. Once established, the rooted section
          can be separated and planted independently.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Bulbs & Offsets</h3>
        <p>
          Many bulbs naturally produce offsets or
          smaller bulbs around the original plant.
          These can often be separated and replanted
          when dormant.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Transplanting Young Plants</h3>
        <p>
          Handle young plants carefully, protect their
          roots from drying out, and water thoroughly
          after transplanting to help them establish.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="pests">
    <p class="eyebrow">Protecting Garden Health</p>
    <h2>Pest & Disease Management</h2>

    <p>
      Healthy gardens depend on maintaining a balance
      between plants, beneficial organisms, and the
      surrounding environment. Regular observation
      helps identify potential problems before they
      become severe.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Identifying Garden Pests</h3>
        <p>
          Aphids, spider mites, caterpillars, slugs,
          and other pests can damage leaves, flowers,
          stems, and roots. Examine plants regularly
          for feeding damage and unusual growth.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Beneficial Insects</h3>
        <p>
          Lady beetles, lacewings, hoverflies, and
          other beneficial insects help control
          certain garden pests. Flowering plants
          provide food and habitat for these helpful
          garden visitors.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Common Plant Diseases</h3>
        <p>
          Fungal diseases, powdery mildew, leaf spots,
          and root problems can affect garden plants.
          Appropriate spacing, airflow, good drainage,
          and suitable watering practices help reduce
          many disease problems.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Integrated Pest Management</h3>
        <p>
          Monitor plants, identify the actual problem,
          encourage natural predators, and use physical
          or cultural controls first. If a pesticide
          is necessary, select an appropriate product
          and follow its label directions carefully.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="weeds">
    <p class="eyebrow">Maintaining Healthy Garden Beds</p>
    <h2>Weed Management</h2>

    <p>
      Weeds compete with garden plants for water,
      nutrients, sunlight, and growing space.
      Regular maintenance helps prevent weeds
      from becoming established and spreading.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Hand Weeding</h3>
        <p>
          Remove weeds while they are young and before
          they produce seeds. Where possible, remove
          the roots of perennial weeds to prevent
          regrowth.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Mulch & Ground Cover</h3>
        <p>
          Covering bare soil with suitable mulch or
          established ground-cover plants can reduce
          the space available for weed germination.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Preventing Weed Spread</h3>
        <p>
          Remove seed heads before they mature and
          clean garden tools when moving between
          areas where invasive weeds may be present.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Garden Bed Maintenance</h3>
        <p>
          Regularly inspect borders, pathways, and
          planting areas. Consistent small maintenance
          tasks are generally easier than allowing
          weeds to become established.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="seasonal">
    <p class="eyebrow">A Year of Gardening</p>
    <h2>Seasonal Garden Maintenance</h2>

    <p>
      Each season brings different gardening tasks.
      A seasonal routine helps keep plants healthy,
      maintain garden structure, and prepare the
      landscape for changing weather conditions.
    </p>

    <div class="season-grid">
      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Spring</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Clear winter debris from garden beds.</li>
          <li>Prepare soil and add suitable compost.</li>
          <li>Divide appropriate herbaceous perennials.</li>
          <li>Plant suitable trees, shrubs, and flowers.</li>
          <li>Inspect irrigation systems.</li>
          <li>Apply mulch where needed.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Summer</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Monitor soil moisture and water deeply.</li>
          <li>Deadhead flowering plants where appropriate.</li>
          <li>Harvest vegetables and herbs regularly.</li>
          <li>Monitor for pests and plant diseases.</li>
          <li>Remove weeds before they set seed.</li>
          <li>Maintain pathways and garden edges.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Autumn</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Plant suitable spring-flowering bulbs.</li>
          <li>Plant trees and shrubs where conditions permit.</li>
          <li>Collect leaves for composting or leaf mould.</li>
          <li>Remove diseased plant material.</li>
          <li>Protect vulnerable plants as necessary.</li>
          <li>Prepare irrigation systems for winter.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Winter</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Inspect trees and shrubs for storm damage.</li>
          <li>Protect vulnerable plants from harsh weather.</li>
          <li>Clean and maintain garden tools.</li>
          <li>Review the previous growing season.</li>
          <li>Plan new garden beds and planting combinations.</li>
          <li>Order seeds and prepare for spring.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-section" id="okanagan">
    <p class="eyebrow">Gardening in a Dry Summer Climate</p>
    <h2>Garden Care in the Okanagan</h2>

    <p>
      Gardening in the Okanagan requires an understanding
      of hot, dry summers, seasonal rainfall, local soil
      conditions, and winter temperature fluctuations.
      Good garden management helps conserve water while
      maintaining healthy and attractive planting areas.
    </p>

    <div class="care-grid">
      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Water Conservation</h3>
        <p>
          Use drip irrigation, water deeply when needed,
          group plants according to their water requirements,
          and apply suitable mulch to reduce evaporation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Choosing Suitable Plants</h3>
        <p>
          Select plants suited to local hardiness,
          sunlight, soil conditions, and available
          irrigation. Once established, many adapted
          plants require less supplemental water.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Soil Improvement</h3>
        <p>
          Organic matter can improve soil structure
          and water retention. Use compost and other
          appropriate amendments according to soil
          conditions and plant requirements.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="care-card">
        <h3>Summer Garden Maintenance</h3>
        <p>
          Monitor newly planted trees and shrubs,
          inspect irrigation coverage, remove weeds,
          and pay attention to signs of heat or
          moisture stress.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="care-closing">
    <p class="eyebrow">A Garden to Enjoy for Years to Come</p>
    <h2>The Rewards of Good Garden Care</h2>
    <p>
      A well-maintained garden is the result of many
      thoughtful decisions made throughout the year.
      By caring for the soil, understanding plant needs,
      conserving water, and maintaining a seasonal
      routine, gardeners can create outdoor spaces
      that remain healthy, welcoming, and beautiful.
    </p>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .care-intro {
    max-width: 850px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .care-intro > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .care-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: .65rem;
    margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .care-nav a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: .5rem .9rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 20px;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    font-size: .9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .care-nav a:hover {
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .care-section {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 14px;
    box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgba(90, 50, 65, .04);
    scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .care-section h2,
  .care-closing h2 {
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    margin-top: .25rem;
  }

  .care-section > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .care-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .care-card {
    padding: 1.2rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  .care-card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
  }

  .care-card p {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 0;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .care-note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 6px;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .season-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .season-card {
    padding: 1.4rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  .season-card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
  }

  .season-card ul {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .season-card li {
    margin-bottom: .4rem;
  }

  .care-closing {
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  .care-closing p:last-child {
    max-width: 700px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .care-section {
      padding: 1.25rem;
    }

    .care-grid,
    .season-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .care-nav {
      gap: .5rem;
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .care-nav a {
      font-size: .85rem;
    }

    .care-closing {
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
